  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 447 AM MST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nSNOW SHOWERS TODAY:\n\nA fast-moving trough will track across eastern Utah and western\nColorado today and tonight with the trough axis pushing across \nthe Divide during evening rush hour. Southwest flow will focus \nprecipitation across the Four Corners with the San Juans being \nfavored for accumulating snowfall before the low lifts across \nthe central mountain corridor with some wraparound precipitation\npossible over the central mountains tonight. A rain/snow mix is\nexpected over the valleys, becoming all rain as temperatures\nwarm throughout the day. Steep lapse rates this afternoon will \npromote periods of heavier snow showers across the mountains at \ntimes, but overall accumulations look to remain below advisory \nlevels in the 2 to 5-inch range. Messy travel conditions may\ndevelop at pass level, especially late this afternoon into \nthis evening. Exercise caution if traveling!\n\nDRY WEATHER THROUGH MIDWEEK, POTENTIALLY ACTIVE AGAIN LATE WEEK:\n\nSkies will clear by Tuesday morning with northerly flow behind \nthe departing trough as a high pressure ridge moves in from the \nwest. This will lead to dry conditions and much colder \ntemperatures Tuesday night as efficient radiational cooling \ntakes place. A weak shortwave trough looks to move through on \nThanksgiving Day but QG forcing shows signs of splitting with \nnot much impact to the area. With a large area of high pressure \nbuilding off the SoCal coast and Desert Southwest, the northern \nstream looks to remain active with systems moving through the \nPacific Northwest and Northern Rockies. This potentially keeps \nnorthwest flow across the area, potentially feeding moisture \ninto the Park Range for favored orographics late this week into \nthe coming weekend. There is some disagreement on how this \nevolves but potential exists for active weather during this \ntimeframe with impacts particularly to the Park Range. Overall \nwith ridging mid to late week, temperatures look to warm up to \naround 5 to 10 degrees above normal.",
